The code mints certificates unattended until it expires; enroll again before
then to keep the job running. If the approval above was not yours, say so now:
the address it was approved from is what says whose session was used.

SETTING THE JOB UP

Add this to the ~/.ssh/config of the account that runs the job, replacing
/path/to/key with the private key you enrolled:

  Match user {{ if .ServiceAccount }}{{ .ServiceAccount }}{{ else }}USERNAME{{ end }} exec 'ssoossh service retrieve --code CODE --key /path/to/key'
    IdentityFile /path/to/key
    IdentitiesOnly yes

`Match user` names the account being connected to, which is always the service
account above: it is the sole principal, so ssh cannot present the certificate
as anyone else.

No CertificateFile line is needed. ssh derives the certificate's name from
IdentityFile, so the three files must be <name>, <name>.pub and <name>-cert.pub
in one directory. Match exec runs before ssh reads them, so this needs no agent.

The certificate is refreshed when it has expired or expires within a minute.
Change that with --grace <time>, or refresh unconditionally with --force.
<time> is a number followed by s, m, or h. The code is reusable until it
expires, so the same command is safe from cron or a systemd timer.
